file the Certificate of Revival with Delaware.

.why = this document formally revives your void corporation. once processed, your corp is restored "as if it was never voided."

---

## ⛔ PRECONDITION — this certificate is a ONE-WAY DOOR on your right to dispute

**§ 505(b) is the only petition route a void corporation still has, and its
window closes the moment this stone executes.**

the statute gives two petition paths, and they are bounded differently — one by
the calendar, one by **this filing**:

> "(a) If any corporation claims that the annual franchise tax or any penalties
> or interest were **erroneously or illegally fixed or paid** with respect to a
> calendar year, the corporation may, **not later than March 1 of the second
> calendar year following the close of such calendar year**, petition the
> Secretary of State for a reduction or refund of such tax, penalties or
> interest."
>
> "(b) **Prior to the filing of a certificate required by § 312(c)** of this
> title, a corporation may petition the Secretary of State for a reduction of
> taxes, penalties or interest which the State claims are due it pursuant to
> **§ 312(g)** of this title and which the corporation claims have been
> erroneously or illegally fixed."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 505(a)–(b), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

⚠️ **"a certificate required by § 312(c)" is THIS certificate.** § 312(c) reads
*"by executing, acknowledging and filing a certificate of revival in accordance
with § 103 of this title."* there is no other candidate.

| path | its bound | for a corporation void ~2 years |
|---|---|---|
| **§ 505(a)** | a **date** — March 1 of the second calendar year after the tax year | ⛔ already expired for the oldest delinquent years |
| **§ 505(b)** | an **event** — before the revival certificate | ✅ open **until this stone runs**, then gone |

⇒ so the two windows fail in opposite ways, and a delinquent corporation is
usually past (a) before it ever learns (b) exists. **(b) is the whole remedy,
and this stone spends it.**

### 🎯 the order this imposes

```
0. substantiate, if the Division asked      <- §503(f) supplemental affidavit
1. file the delinquent federal 1120s
2. AMEND each filed year / file each absent one
   └─ displaces the §502(c) assessment; SERIALIZE, one year at a time
3. pay
   └─ ⛔ if the number is still wrong, PETITION UNDER §505(b) FIRST
4. THEN file this certificate               <- the door shuts here
```

⚠️ **steps 0 and 2 were absent from this block until 2026-09-14**, and their
absence was the sharpest kind: **the facts were already in this file.** the
§ 502(c) `$200`-per-year report penalty sits ~360 lines below; the attestation
appears ten times further down still. **a reader acts on the order block, and
the order block did not know what the rest of the file knew.** see
`3.pay.taxes.stone`, which carries the full statement of the order and why each
gate exists.

⭐ **the petition is insurance, not a detour.** where step 2 worked and the
figure is right, you never file one. it exists for the case where the Division
declines to displace the assessment, or displaces it to a number you dispute —
and that case is only survivable if you learn of it **before** step 4.

### ⚠️ and only a COURT can remit the penalties, for a taxable corporation

read the two remission powers against each other. the Secretary's is
**conditioned**; the Court's is not:

> "(c) … In the case of any corporation **which is not required to pay an annual
> tax under § 501(a)** of this title, the Secretary of State **may remit** all or
> part of the penalties and interest provided in this chapter."
>
> "(d) Any corporation, **within a period of 60 days** after the determination by
> the Secretary of State on a petition filed pursuant to subsections (a) and (b)
> of this section, may petition the **Court of Chancery** … for a **review de
> novo** of the determination of the Secretary of State."
>
> "(e) … **The Court of Chancery may remit all or part of the penalties and
> interest provided in § 502** of this title."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 505(c)–(e)

| who | may remit penalties + interest | on what condition |
|---|---|---|
| the Secretary of State | § 505(c) | ⛔ **only** for a corporation exempt under § 501(a) |
| the **Court of Chancery** | § 505(e) | ✅ **no such condition** |

⇒ an ordinary stock corporation is **not** § 501(a)-exempt, so the Secretary has
no power to waive its penalties — a clerk who says *"I can't waive that"* has the
statute right. **the remission power exists one level up**, and § 505(d) is how
you reach it: a **60-day** fuse from the Secretary's determination, and it runs
**de novo**, so the Court re-decides rather than merely reviews.

#### ✅ and § 501 settles that by TEXT, not by intuition — check it once, then stop

it is worth one pull, because the whole § 505(c)/(e) split turns on it and the
natural reflex is to ask the Secretary anyway:

> "(a) Every telegraph, telephone or cable company, every electric company
> organized for the production and/or distribution of light, heat or power, …
> and **every corporation now existing or hereafter to be incorporated under the
> laws of this State, shall pay an annual tax**, for the use of the State, by way
> of license for the corporate franchise as prescribed in this chapter. **No such
> tax shall be paid by any exempt corporation**, any banking corporation, savings
> bank, building and loan association or any captive insurance company licensed
> under Chapter 69 of Title 18, or any corporation for drainage and reclamation
> of lowlands."
>
> "(b) As used in this chapter, the term "exempt corporation" shall be defined as
> **any corporation organized under Chapter 1 of this title that**: (1) **Is
> exempt from taxation under § 501(c) of the United States Internal Revenue
> Code** (26 U.S.C. § 501(c)) or any similar provisions…; (2) Qualifies as a
> civic organization under § 8110(a)(1) of Title 9 or § 6840 of Title 16; (3)
> Qualifies as a charitable/fraternal organization under § 2593(1) of Title 6;
> (4) Is listed in § 8106(a) of Title 9; (5) Is organized primarily or
> exclusively for religious or charitable purposes…; or (6) a. **Is organized not
> for profit**; and b. No part of its net earnings inures to the benefit of any
> member or individual."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 501(a)–(b), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

### 🚩 RETRACTED — both quotes above were wrong until 2026-09-14

> 🚩 this section carried a **fabricated** § 501(b) and a **truncated** § 501(a).
> caught by `rhx quote.verify` on its first live run, against the chapter-5
> bytes already on disk the whole time.
>
> **(b) was invented outright.** it read *"The following corporations are exempt
> from the tax imposed by this chapter: (1) Corporations organized under Chapter
> 3 of Title 12; (2) Corporations not organized for profit…; (3) Religious
> corporations; (4) Corporations for charitable purposes; (5) Corporations for
> educational purposes; (6) Civic organizations…"* — **no clause of that is in
> the statute.** the real (b) does not list categories at all; it **defines the
> term "exempt corporation"** by cross-reference to IRC § 501(c) and to Titles 6,
> 9, and 16.
>
> **(a) was truncated with no ellipsis to mark it.** it opened *"Every
> corporation now existing or hereafter…"* — a **mid-sentence clause**, lifted
> out of a long antecedent list of utility, transport, and insurance companies,
> with its lower-case "every" capitalized to pose as the start of the subsection.
> the second sentence — *"No such tax shall be paid by any exempt corporation…"*,
> the operative exemption — was dropped entirely.
>
> ⚠️ **the gloss beneath it was approximately true, which is why no check ever
> fired.** "(b) is a closed list of six" is the right shape and the wrong
> mechanism: the six are **conditions on a definition**, not a list of exempt
> corporations, and (b) alone exempts no one — it is **(a)'s second sentence**
> that does the exempting, and that sentence was absent from the page.

⭐ **the conclusion survives the correction, and is now better grounded.** (a)
reaches "every corporation now existing or hereafter to be incorporated under
the laws of this State" and relieves only an **exempt corporation** — a term (b)
defines as one organized under Chapter 1 that is IRC § 501(c)-exempt, civic,
charitable/fraternal, religious, or not-for-profit with no earnings inuring to a
member. a for-profit stock corporation satisfies no branch.

so § 505(c) is unavailable **by statute**, and an ask to the Secretary to waive
penalties is a request they lack the power to grant — not a negotiation you lost.

⇒ **budget the penalties as payable, and treat § 505(e) as the only waiver
route.** it reaches the same figures, and it is a court.

---

## ⛔ DO NOT reach for a CERTIFICATE OF VALIDATION here — § 312(g) makes it the most expensive filing in this chapter

§ 505(a)'s closing sentence names two instruments; `3.pay.taxes.stone` carries
the § 103(f) half. **§ 204 is the half that lands on THIS step**, and it carries
a second cost far larger than the barred refund.

§ 204 is the ratification statute for a **defective corporate act** — an act that
is void or voidable for a *failure of authorization*:

> "**"Failure of authorization" means:** (i) the failure to authorize or effect
> an act or transaction in compliance with (A) the provisions of this title, (B)
> the certificate of incorporation or bylaws of the corporation, or (C) any plan
> or agreement to which the corporation is a party or the disclosure set forth
> in any proxy or consent solicitation statement, **if and to the extent such
> failure would render such act or transaction void or voidable**; or (ii) **the
> failure of the board of directors or any officer of the corporation to
> authorize or approve any act or transaction** taken by or on behalf of the
> corporation that would have required for its due authorization the approval of
> the board of directors or such officer;"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 204(h)(2), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc06/index.html

> ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-14** — `sc06` pulled via bhrowser (headful) and verified
> against `.scratch/refs/pulls/delcode.title8.c001.sc06.txt` (52,583 chars).
>
> 🚩 **the pull found three defects in the quote above, and the worst was a
> dropped condition.**
>
> | what it said | what § 204(h)(2) says |
> |---|---|
> | `'Failure of authorization'` | `"Failure of authorization"` — double quotes |
> | "…to which the corporation is a party **…**" | "…is a party **or the disclosure set forth in any proxy or consent solicitation statement**" |
> | *(the clause ended there)* | "**, if and to the extent such failure would render such act or transaction void or voidable**" |
>
> ⛔ **the third row is the one that changes the answer.** as quoted, prong (i)
> read as an unqualified catch-all: any deviation from the certificate or bylaws
> is a failure of authorization. the statute limits it to failures that **render
> the act void or voidable** — a materially narrower class, and the limit a
> founder would reach for first when asked whether § 204 applies at all.
>
> ⭐ **it was flagged ⬜ OPEN for exactly one day before the pull closed it**, and
> the flag is why the defect was found. the prior version of this claim carried
> no flag, which reads as *verified* to anyone who does not re-run the checker.

🚩 **a sole-founder corporation with a thin minute book is squarely inside
(ii)** — an act taken with no board resolution behind it. so this is not a remote
statute; it is the one a founder is most likely to be pointed at.

### 🎯 the trigger is the DATE OF THE ACT, not the date you file

read § 312(g)'s last sentence with care. the operative words are **"relates to a
time during which"**:

> "**If the filing of a certificate of validation under § 204 of this title
> relates to a time during which the corporation's certificate of incorporation
> had been forfeited or void**, the annual reports and annual franchise taxes
> that would have been required to be filed and paid during the period in which
> the corporation's certificate of incorporation had been forfeited or void,
> including interest thereon, **are required to be filed and paid at the time of
> the filing of such certificate of validation**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(g), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

| the act you would ratify happened… | what § 204 costs under § 312(g) |
|---|---|
| **before** the charter went void | the § 312(g) re-opener does not fire — the certificate relates to a time of good standing |
| **while** the charter was void | ⛔ **the § 312(g) cap is lifted.** every void-year report, every void-year tax, and interest on all of it, due at the moment you file |

⭐ **§ 312(g) otherwise caps the revival payment at the balance owed on the void
date.** that cap is the single largest concession in the revival statute, and
**§ 204 is the one act that surrenders it.**

### 🚩 row 1 is NOT a safe harbor — § 503(e) uses a DIFFERENT KEY

> the table above was complete as to § 312(g) and read as
> complete **full stop**. a second subsection reaches the same certificate on a
> **different trigger**, and it catches the row the table calls safe.

> "The **filing of a certificate of validation under § 204** of this title
> **shall not reduce** the annual franchise tax and interest due for **any
> period prior to the filing of such certificate of validation**, and **shall
> not entitle the corporation to a refund**, and any calculation of
> **additional** annual franchise tax and interest due for any period prior to
> the filing of such certificate of validation shall be calculated **at the
> current rates in effect under this section**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 503(e), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

put the two keys side by side:

| provision | what it keys on | reach |
|---|---|---|
| **§ 312(g)** | the **act's** date — *"relates to a time **during which**"* the charter was void | the void window only |
| **§ 503(e)** | the **certificate's** date — *"any period **prior to the filing** of such certificate"* | ⛔ **every prior period.** void or not |

⭐ **so § 503(e) is the broader of the two, and it is the one that makes row 1
false.** a validation of a pre-void act escapes the § 312(g) re-opener and still
cannot reduce a penny of prior-year tax, still earns no refund, and still prices
any *additional* tax **at today's rates rather than that year's**.

⛔ **there is no version of § 204 that lowers a back-year franchise tax.** three
provisions say so, and each closes a different door:

| provision | the door it closes |
|---|---|
| § 503(e) | no reduction · no refund · additional tax at **current rates** |
| § 312(g) | the revival **cap** re-opens for a void-window act |
| § 505(a) | *"No refund shall be granted in connection with the filing of a certificate of correction under § 103(f) ... or ... validation under § 204."* |

⚠️ **and note what § 503(e) does NOT bar: an INCREASE.** it forecloses down and
expressly contemplates *"additional ... tax"*. **the instrument is
one-directional against you.**

### ⭐ and for a void-window act you almost certainly do not NEED § 204

§ 312(e) already validates those acts, retroactively, at no extra charge:

> "Such revival shall **validate all contracts, acts, matters and things** made,
> done and performed within the scope of its certificate of incorporation by the
> corporation, its directors or members of its governing body, officers, agents
> and stockholders or members **during the time when its certificate of
> incorporation was forfeited or void** pursuant to this title, with the same
> force and effect and to all intents and purposes as if the certificate of
> incorporation **had at all times remained in full force and effect**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(e)

> 🚩 **this quote was TRUNCATED with no ellipsis until 2026-09-14.** it read
> *"its directors, officers, agents and stockholders"* and dropped the statute's
> *"or members of its governing body"* and *"or members"* — the nonstock
> alternates — and it elided *"with the same force and effect and to all intents
> and purposes"* behind a `…` that carried more weight than an ellipsis may.
> caught by `rhx quote.verify`. **the conclusion is unchanged** (a stock
> corporation's directors and stockholders are squarely inside the clause), which
> is precisely why no read ever queried it.

so the two overlap on exactly the same set of acts, and one of them is free:

```
an act done while void
  ├─ cured by § 312(e) on revival          -> $0
  └─ cured by a § 204 certificate          -> the ENTIRE void window, + interest
```

🚩 **the open question, stated BARE.** § 312(e) validates an act *"within the
scope of its certificate of incorporation."* that phrase speaks to corporate
**power**, which is what § 510 made inoperative. whether it also reaches a
**failure of authorization** — no board resolution behind an act that was
otherwise within the charter — is **not settled by the text pulled here.** the
two defects are different, and an act done while void with no resolution behind
it carries both.

⛔ **do not settle that by reasoning.** it is the question to put to counsel
before any § 204 filing is contemplated, and the cost of the wrong answer is the
whole § 312(g) cap.

### ⭐ and a COUNTERPARTY cannot undo those acts at all — § 124 closed that door

the fear that drives most § 204 questions is not the Division. it is the
customer, vendor, or investor who learns the charter was void and wants out of a
deal. **Delaware abolished that move**, and the abolition does not wait for your
revival:

> "**No act of a corporation and no conveyance or transfer** of real or personal
> property to or by a corporation **shall be invalid by reason of the fact that
> the corporation was without capacity or power** to do such act or to make or
> receive such conveyance or transfer, **but such lack of capacity or power may
> be asserted**:
>
> (1) **In a proceeding by a stockholder** against the corporation to enjoin the
> doing of any act ...;
>
> (2) **In a proceeding by the corporation**, whether acting directly or through
> a receiver, trustee or other legal representative, or through stockholders in a
> representative suit, **against an incumbent or former officer or director** ...
> **for loss or damage** due to such ... unauthorized act;
>
> (3) **In a proceeding by the Attorney General** to dissolve the corporation, or
> to enjoin the corporation from the transaction of unauthorized business."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 124, https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc02/index.html

> ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-14** — `sc02` pulled via bhrowser (headful) and verified
> against `.scratch/refs/pulls/delcode.title8.c001.sc02.txt` (17,369 chars).
> the quote is **byte-faithful**; no alteration was found.
>
> ⚠️ **but read what the ellipsis in (1) hides, because the list below leans on
> it.** the full text of (1) continues:
>
> > "…If the unauthorized acts or transfer sought to be enjoined are being, or
> > are to be, performed or made pursuant to any contract to which the
> > corporation is a party, **the court may, if all of the parties to the
> > contract are parties to the proceeding** and if it deems the same to be
> > equitable, **set aside and enjoin the performance of such contract**, and in
> > so doing may allow to the corporation or to the other parties to the
> > contract … such compensation as may be equitable for the loss or damage
> > sustained by any of them … but **anticipated profits** to be derived from
> > the performance of the contract **shall not be awarded**"
>
> ⭐ **so the list is closed as to WHO may raise it, and open as to WHAT a court
> may then do.** the conclusion below survives — a counterparty still cannot
> assert your lack of capacity — but a **stockholder** who does assert it can
> have the contract set aside, and the counterparty is then drawn into that
> proceeding as a necessary party. at a sole-stockholder corporation the only
> person with standing to do that is the founder, which is why this stays a
> reassurance here. ⛔ **it would not be one at a corporation with outside
> holders**, and this route's template will be read by such corporations.
>
> 🚩 **the flag that produced this read was up for one day.** the prior version
> carried no flag, and the asymmetry is why that mattered: this section tells a
> founder a counterparty *cannot* undo a void-window deal, and a reader acts on
> a reassurance by **inaction** — by refusal to renegotiate, or by a position
> held in a dispute. an unverified reassurance is more dangerous than an
> unverified caution, because the error surfaces only once the deal is already
> contested.

⛔ **read the list as CLOSED, and read who is on it.** three parties may raise
lack of capacity, and a **counterparty is not one of them**:

| who wants out of the deal | may they assert the void? |
|---|---|
| your customer, vendor, landlord, or investor | ⛔ **no** — not on the list |
| a **stockholder**, and only to **enjoin** a still-unperformed act | ✅ (1) |
| the **corporation** — or a receiver in its name — against **an officer or director** | ⚠️ (2) |
| the **Attorney General**, to dissolve or enjoin | ✅ (3) |

⭐ **so the completed acts of the void window are not voidable by the people you
did business with.** § 124 says so on its own terms, before § 312(e) adds
retroactive validation on top. two independent grounds, one of which needs no
filing at all.

⚠️ **but read row (2) as the exposure it is.** it runs **against the officer**,
in the corporation's name — and § 509 lets the Attorney General petition for a
**receiver** who would then control whether that suit is brought. those two
sections compose, and the composition is the one civil exposure a void window
actually creates for the human who signed.

⚠️ **§ 124 is civil only.** it says which *proceedings* may assert incapacity,
and is silent as to the criminal penalty in § 513. see the § 511 question in
`2.call.delaware.stone`.

⚠️ **if the Division or a counterparty asks for a certificate of validation,
treat it as a material change of scope** — re-price the matter before you agree.

⚠️ § 505(d) opens *"on a petition filed pursuant to subsections (a) and (b)"* —
so **the Chancery route requires a § 505 petition to have been filed first.**
skip the petition and you lose the appeal with it.

🚩 **the penalties are real and per-year:** § 502(c) adds **$200** for each year a
complete annual report was absent, and § 504(c) runs interest at **1½% per month
or portion thereof** until paid. those are the figures § 505(e) reaches.

