## form

official name: Certificate of Revival of Charter for a Voided Corporation

> ⚠️ **a clerk may call it a "Certificate of RENEWAL", the fee schedule row
> reads `Revival (plus taxes to renew)`, and the form itself says "Revival".
> one instrument, three names. the statute behind it is § 312 — NOT § 314.**
>
> the word "renew" is the trap, and it is baited three ways at once:
>
> - the **subchapter heading** is *"Renewal, Revival, Extension and Restoration
>   of Certificate of Incorporation or Charter"* — it sits above **§ 311**, and
>   promises a "renewal" section that does not exist
> - the **fee schedule** row is literally `Revival (plus taxes to renew)`
> - a full-text search for `renew` inside that subchapter lands on **§ 314**
>   ahead of the rest, because § 314 is the only one whose body opens with the
>   word: *"Any corporation **desiring to renew**, extend and continue its
>   corporate existence shall..."*
>
> ⛔ **§ 314 is captioned "Status of corporation" and prescribes no procedure at
> all** — no petition, no filing, no contents, no clock. it says such a
> corporation *"shall continue **as provided in its certificate effecting the
> foregoing**"*, which presupposes a certificate filed under some other section
> and never says which. follow the word "renew" to § 314 and you find no route,
> from which it is easy to conclude the path is shut.
>
> 🚩 **§ 311 is the more expensive wrong door**, because it *does* prescribe a
> procedure — one a voided corporation does not owe. it reaches a **§ 275
> voluntary dissolution**, or a certificate that **expired by its own
> limitation**, and for those it demands a board resolution, a special
> stockholder meeting on § 222 notice, and a vote. a § 510 tax void is neither.
>
> ✅ **§ 312(b) is the door, and it names the condition in terms:** *"Any
> corporation whose certificate of incorporation has become **forfeited or void
> pursuant to this title** may **at any time** procure a revival."* note **"at
> any time"** — no deadline governs this filing.
>
> ⚠️ its one carve-out sits in the same subsection: *"this section shall not be
> applicable to a corporation whose certificate of incorporation has been
> revoked or forfeited **pursuant to § 284**"* — a Court of Chancery revocation
> for abuse of corporate powers. a § 510 tax void is not that.
>
> | § | caption | what it actually reaches |
> |---|---|---|
> | 311 | Revocation of voluntary dissolution; restoration of expired certificate | a § 275 dissolution, or expiry by the certificate's own limitation ⛔ |
> | **312** | **Revival of certificate of incorporation** | ✅ **a charter forfeited or void under this title — the § 510 case** |
> | 313 | Revival … of exempt corporations | ⛔ only where the corporation is exempt |
> | 314 | Status of corporation | states an effect; ⛔ prescribes no procedure |
>
> ⇒ in the form catalogue the branch is **Corporation → Revival for Void**, two
> hops below. source bytes:
> `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/de-title8-ch1-sc12-revival.txt`
> — §§ 311/312/313/314 at lines 6/49/84/95, read from disk.

the four sections of subchapter XII, in their own words. read them and the
naming collision resolves itself:

> "Any corporation whose certificate of incorporation has become forfeited or
> void pursuant to this title may at any time procure a revival of its
> certificate of incorporation, together with all the rights, franchises,
> privileges and immunities and subject to all of its duties, debts and
> liabilities which had been secured or imposed by its original certificate of
> incorporation and all amendments thereto, by complying with the requirements
> of this section. Notwithstanding the foregoing, this section shall not be
> applicable to a corporation whose certificate of incorporation has been
> revoked or forfeited pursuant to § 284 of this title."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(b),
> https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

> "Any corporation desiring to renew, extend and continue its corporate
> existence shall, upon complying with applicable constitutional provisions of
> this State, continue as provided in its certificate effecting the foregoing
> as a corporation and shall, in addition to the rights, privileges and
> immunities conferred by its charter, possess and enjoy all the benefits of
> this chapter, which are applicable to the nature of its business, and shall
> be subject to the restrictions and liabilities by this chapter imposed on
> such corporations."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 314, **in full** — that is the entire section,
> https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

⚠️ § 314 is quoted **whole** on purpose. the claim above is that it prescribes
no procedure, and a claim about what a section **omits** cannot be proven by an
excerpt — only by the whole of it.

and § 311, the costly wrong door:

> "a corporation may revoke the dissolution theretofore effected by it or
> restore its certificate of incorporation after it has expired by its own
> limitation in the following manner:"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 311(a),
> https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

> "The board of directors shall adopt a resolution recommending that the
> dissolution be revoked in the case of a dissolution or that the certificate
> of incorporation be restored in the case of an expiration by limitation and
> directing that the question of the revocation or restoration be submitted to
> a vote at a special meeting of stockholders."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 311(a)(2),
> https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

⇒ **"revoke the dissolution" and "restore ... after it has expired by its own
limitation"** are the only two states § 311 reaches. a § 510 tax void is
neither, so the board resolution and the special stockholder meeting that
§ 311(a)(2) demands are **not owed** on this route.

