  -> corp.delaware.gov/formsdoctype09/  ->  "Revivals"
    -> corp.delaware.gov/renew09/  ->  Corporation / Revival for Void
      -> corpfiles.delaware.gov/Corp_Forms/Revival%20void.pdf
```

the file harvested by nav is stamped **`rev. 08/2023`**, 217,343 bytes, and
its own text states the **current $189.00** fee.

> 🚨 **this stone used to point at `renewalvoid16.pdf` — a url nobody
> harvested — and then cautioned that the form's own fee was a stale `$169`.**
>
> both halves came from the same defect. the `16` in that slug matches the
> `rev. 08/16` stamp: it was an old revision, reached by a constructed path.
> the **navigated** form carries the right fee, so the caution existed only
> because the url was guessed.
>
> ⭐ a guessed url does not merely 404. it can **succeed, at a stale revision**,
> and then every fact read off it is wrong in a way no byte count and no
> soft-404 check can see. `rule.forbid.guessed-urls`

⚠️ still read the fee off the **fee schedule** under `## fees`, not off the
form — a form is a snapshot, the schedule is the authority.

---

## required information

> ✅ **grounded.** this table was correct and uncited. § 312(d) states
> the contents by statute, so each row now carries the clause that demands it.

> "(d) The certificate required by subsection (c) of this section **shall
> state**:
>
> (1) **The date of filing of the corporation's original certificate of
> incorporation**; the name under which the corporation was originally
> incorporated; the name of the corporation at the time its certificate of
> incorporation became forfeited or void ...;
>
> (2) The address ... of the corporation's **registered office** in this State
> and the **name of its registered agent** at such address;
>
> (3) That the corporation ... **was organized under the laws of this State**;
>
> (4) **The date when the certificate of incorporation became forfeited or
> void** ...; and
>
> (5) That the certificate of revival is **filed by authority of the board of
> directors** ... in accordance with subsection (h) of this section."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(d), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

| field | value | source | § 312(d) |
|-------|-------|--------|---|
| corporation name — **original**, and **as of the void date** | | 0.gather.position.yield.md | (d)(1) |
| file number | | 0.gather.position.yield.md | — administrative |
| date of incorporation — ⚠️ **the FILING date, see § 106 below** | | 0.gather.position.yield.md | (d)(1) |
| ⛔ **date voided** | | 0.gather.position.yield.md | **(d)(4)** |
| registered agent name | | current or new agent | (d)(2) |
| registered agent address | | Delaware address required | (d)(2) |
| organized under Delaware law | | attestation on the form | (d)(3) |
| signature | | ⚠️ **see § 312(h) below** | (d)(5) |

### 🚩 (d)(4) makes the VOID DATE a required field — confirm it, do not derive it

⚠️ **a void date you reasoned your way to is not good enough for (d)(4).** the
ordinary derivation runs: tax due March 1 → § 510's one year of neglect → the
mandatory November 30 notice → void *"on or before March 1 of the following
year."* that chain is sound **and it is not the record**.

| why a derived date fails here | |
|---|---|
| § 510 grants the Secretary of State a **good-cause extension** — *"unless the Secretary of State, for good cause shown, shall have given further time"* | moves the date **later**, invisibly |
| the **unfiled-report** prong can bite before the tax prong | moves it **earlier** |
| the certificate is filed under § 103 and is **sworn** | a wrong date is a wrong sworn statement |

🎯 **get the date from Delaware.** the entity page or the franchise tax line will
state it. this is a one-question call and it unblocks a required field.

### 🚩 (d)(1) wants the FILING date — § 106, never the signature-line date

read (d)(1) again, word for word: *"The date of **filing** of the corporation's
original certificate of incorporation."* that is a narrower field than it looks,
because a charter carries **two** dates and they need not be the same one:

| the date | where it appears | is it the (d)(1) field? |
|---|---|---|
| **executed / acknowledged** | the signature block, per § 103 | ⛔ no |
| **filed** | the Secretary of State's stamp and the entity record | ✅ yes |

§ 106 is the section that makes the filing date the operative one:

> "Upon the **filing** with the Secretary of State of the certificate of
> incorporation, executed and acknowledged in accordance with § 103 of this
> title, the incorporator or incorporators who signed the certificate ... shall,
> **from the date of such filing**, be and constitute a body corporate, by the
> name set forth in the certificate, **subject to § 103(d)** of this title ..."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 106, https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc01/index.html

⭐ corporate existence **commences at filing**, never at execution. so a charter
signed one day and filed the next describes a corporation whose (d)(1) date is
the second one, and whose first day of existence is the second one too.

⚠️ **and § 106 is expressly subject to § 103(d), which is a real second branch:**

> "Any instrument filed in accordance with subsection (c) of this section shall
> be effective upon its filing date. Any instrument **may provide that it is not
> to become effective until a specified time subsequent to the time it is
> filed**, but such time shall not be later than a time on the **ninetieth day**
> after the date of its filing."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(d)

a charter that elects a delayed effective time therefore has a filing date and a
**later** existence date, as much as 90 days apart. (d)(1) still asks for the
**filing** date — but do not assume the two coincide until you have read the face
of the certificate.

🎯 **the check is one read of two documents**, and it costs no call:

| document | what it prints |
|---|---|
| the agency's **entity record** | the filing date — this is the (d)(1) answer |
| the face of the **charter** | the execution date, and any elected future effective time |

⚠️ a one- or two-day gap between those two is **ordinary**, not a defect in your
records. it is the mail or the queue. the field takes the agency's date; a gap
you can name and explain never blocks the form.

### ⛔ "FILED" is a two-part act — and the money is half of it

§ 103(d) above says an instrument is effective "upon its **filing date**." it
never defines the word. § 103(c) does, and the definition is not what the plain
word suggests:

> "(c) Whenever any instrument is to be filed with the Secretary of State ...
> such requirement means that: (1) The **signed instrument shall be delivered**
> to the office of the Secretary of State; (2) **All taxes and fees** authorized
> by law to be collected by the Secretary of State in connection with the filing
> of the instrument **shall be tendered** to the Secretary of State; and (3)
> Upon delivery of the instrument, the Secretary of State shall record the date
> and time of its delivery. **Upon such delivery and tender of the required
> taxes and fees**, the Secretary of State shall certify that the instrument has
> been filed in the Secretary of State's office by endorsing upon the signed
> instrument the word "Filed", and the date and time of its filing. This
> endorsement is the "filing date" of the instrument, and is **conclusive of the
> date and time of its filing in the absence of actual fraud**. The Secretary of
> State shall file and index the endorsed instrument. **Except as provided in
> paragraph (c)(4) of this section and in subsection (i) of this section, such
> filing date of an instrument shall be the date and time of delivery of the
> instrument.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(c),
> https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc01/index.html

⭐ **the paper and the payment are one act, not two.** a Certificate of Renewal
delivered without the full tendered amount has not met (c)(2), so it is not
"filed" — and § 103(d)'s effectiveness has no date to attach to.

⚠️ read the trigger clause twice: *"Upon such delivery **and tender of the
required taxes and fees**, the Secretary of State shall certify..."* the duty to
endorse is conditioned on **both**. a partial tender does not buy a partial
file date — it buys none at all.

🎯 **the endorsement is the evidence, and it is conclusive.** *"conclusive of the
date and time of its filing in the absence of actual fraud"* means the endorsed
date is not a fact anyone argues about later. so **scan the endorsed instrument
the day it comes back** and carry that image forward — it is the strongest proof
of the date that exists, and the office will not re-issue it.

⛔ **note what that last sentence defers to.** the delivery-date rule is written
*"Except as provided in paragraph (c)(4) ... and in **subsection (i)**"* — two
named carve-outs, each able to move the date. (c)(4) is the next section below.
**(i) is the one nobody knows exists**, and it is two sections down.

🎯 **this is the citation behind the order of this route.** the recalculation has
to land *before* the renewal certificate, not after, because until the figure is
settled you cannot tender "all taxes and fees" and therefore cannot file. an
attempt to file first and argue the figure later is not an aggressive strategy —
under (c)(2) it is a non-filing.

⚠️ **and it answers a question the fee schedule cannot.** the renewal fee is a
published number; the *tax* is the disputed one. (c)(2) makes no distinction —
it says **all** taxes and fees.

### 🚨 if they REJECT it, a 5-BUSINESS-DAY clock starts — and it starts at THEIR notice

this is the single hardest deadline in the whole route, and it is invisible
until it fires:

> "(4) ... If the Secretary of State **refuses to file any instrument due to an
> error, omission or other imperfection**, the Secretary of State **may hold such
> instrument in suspension**, and in such event, upon delivery of a replacement
> instrument in proper form for filing and tender of the required taxes and fees
> **within 5 business days after notice of such suspension is given to the
> filer**, the Secretary of State shall establish as the filing date of such
> instrument **the date and time that would have been the filing date of the
> rejected instrument had it been accepted for filing**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(c)(4)

| you do | you get |
|---|---|
| deliver a corrected instrument **within 5 business days of their notice** | ⭐ the **original** filing date, as if it had been accepted the first time |
| deliver it on day six | the new date. every day of the void window between them stays a void day |

⚠️ **read "may hold such instrument in suspension." it is DISCRETIONARY.** the
office is not obliged to suspend rather than reject outright, so the cure is a
privilege to ask for, never a right to assume. if a rejection arrives, **ask
whether the instrument is held in suspension** before you ask aught else — the
answer decides whether a clock is already live.

⛔ **and the clock starts when notice reaches "the filer" — which may not be
you.** if the certificate goes in through a filing agent, the agent is the
filer, the notice lands on the agent's desk, and the 5 business days can be
half spent before you are told.

🎯 **so if an agent files it, agree in advance, in a written instruction, that
any suspension or rejection notice is forwarded to you the same day.** that one
sentence is the difference between a 5-day cure and a lost filing date.

### 🚩 the symptom to recognize: a suspended instrument BLOCKS good standing

the same paragraph closes with a consequence that surfaces two steps later,
where nobody is on watch for it:

> "The Secretary of State **shall not issue a certificate of good standing** with
> respect to any corporation with an instrument held in suspension pursuant to
> this subsection."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(c)(4)

⭐ **that produces a false diagnosis.** you file the renewal, you order the good
standing certificate to confirm it worked, and the office declines to issue one.
the natural read is *"the revival failed."* the statutory read is *"an
instrument of yours is in suspension and a 5-business-day clock may be live."*

| the refusal you see | what it may actually mean |
|---|---|
| no good standing certificate issues | ⛔ an instrument is **in suspension** — not that the revival was refused |
| the entity still reads void | the renewal was rejected outright, **without** suspension |

⇒ `5.verify.restoration.stone` carries this as a named cause when the good
standing order comes back empty.

### ⭐ the SECOND way to save a date — § 103(i), the affidavit of extraordinary condition

(c)(4) saves a date after **your** error. § 103(i) saves a date after **their**
outage — and it is the carve-out the delivery-date sentence named above:

> "(i) (1) If: a. Together with the actual delivery of an instrument and tender of
> the required taxes and fees, there is delivered to the Secretary of State a
> **separate affidavit (which in its heading shall be designated as an "affidavit
> of extraordinary condition")** attesting, on the basis of personal knowledge of
> the affiant or a reliable source of knowledge identified in the affidavit, that
> **an earlier effort to deliver such instrument and tender such taxes and fees
> was made in good faith**, specifying the nature, date and time of such good
> faith effort and requesting that the Secretary of State establish such date and
> time as the filing date of such instrument; or b. ... the Secretary of State in
> the Secretary's discretion provides a written waiver of the requirement for
> such an affidavit ...; and c. The Secretary of State determines that an
> extraordinary condition existed at such date and time, that such earlier effort
> was unsuccessful as a result of the existence of such extraordinary condition,
> and that **such actual delivery and tender were made within a reasonable period
> (not to exceed 2 business days) after the cessation of such extraordinary
> condition**, then the Secretary of State may establish such date and time as
> the filing date of such instrument. **No fee shall be paid to the Secretary of
> State for receiving an affidavit of extraordinary condition.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(i)(1)

and (i)(2) defines the trigger far more broadly than the phrase suggests:

> "(2) For purposes of this subsection, an "extraordinary condition" means: any
> emergency resulting from an attack on, invasion or occupation by foreign
> military forces of, or disaster, catastrophe, war or other armed conflict,
> revolution or insurrection, or rioting or civil commotion in, the United States
> or a locality in which the Secretary of State conducts its business ...; **or
> any malfunction or outage of the electrical or telephone service to the
> Secretary of State's office**, or weather or other condition in or about a
> locality in which the Secretary of State conducts its business, as a result of
> which the Secretary of State's office is not open for the purpose of the filing
> of instruments under this chapter **or such filing cannot be effected without
> extraordinary effort**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(i)(2)

⭐ **read the second half, not the first.** the opening clause is war and
catastrophe and reads as inapplicable to anyone. the closing clause is **an
outage at their office**, or a condition under which the filing *"cannot be
effected without extraordinary effort"* — which is an ordinary e-filing failure
or a closed office on a deadline day.

| you hit | you do |
|---|---|
| the portal is down on the day you must file | **record the date, the time, and what failed** — a screenshot, an error page, a call log |
| the office is shut for weather | same. that record is the affidavit's evidence |
| service is restored | deliver the instrument **and the tender** within **2 business days**, with the affidavit |

⚠️ **three hard limits, and each one kills the remedy on its own:**

1. the earlier effort must have been a **real** delivery-and-tender attempt. an
   intent to file is not an effort; a failed attempt that never carried the money
   does not qualify under (c)(2) reasoning either.
2. **2 business days** after the condition ceases. this is tighter than (c)(4)'s
   five, and it runs from the *cessation*, which you must notice yourself — no
   notice is given to you.
3. the Secretary of State **"may"** establish the date. discretionary again, like
   the suspension in (c)(4). neither carve-out is a right.

🎯 **the practical instruction is one line: never let a deadline day pass with an
unrecorded failure.** if a delivery attempt fails for any reason outside your
control, write down the nature, the date, and the time **that day**. that
contemporaneous note is the only thing (i)(1)a can be built from, and it is free
to make.

⛔ **and it is free to ask.** *"No fee shall be paid ... for receiving an
affidavit of extraordinary condition."* the downside of an affidavit that fails
is the fee you did not pay.

### ⚠️ a THIRD date tool, in the first sentence of (c)(4) — forward-date on request

easy to miss, because it sits in front of the refusal clause quoted above:

> "(4) **Upon request made upon or prior to delivery**, the Secretary of State
> may, to the extent deemed practicable, **establish as the filing date of an
> instrument a date and time after its delivery.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(c)(4)

this is **not** the § 103(d) forward-date option. compare them:

| | § 103(d) | § 103(c)(4), first sentence |
|---|---|---|
| what moves | the **effective** date, while the filing date stays at delivery | the **filing date** itself |
| the cap | *"the ninetieth day"* after the filing date | none stated |
| how | a term **in the instrument** | a **request** to the office |
| the office's duty | ministerial | *"may, to the extent deemed practicable"* — discretionary |

⛔ **for this route, neither is wanted.** the whole object is to end the void
window as early as possible, so a later filing date is a cost, never a benefit.
it is here so that nobody reaches for it by mistake, and so that a reader who
sees a filing date *after* their delivery date knows a request under this
sentence is one explanation.

### 💵 and a fee that appears on no schedule — § 103(c)(7), $40 per instrument

> "The fee to such municipalities shall be **$40 for each instrument filed** with
> the Secretary of State in accordance with this section."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(c)(7)

⚠️ **per instrument, not per filing session.** a revival that carries a renewal
certificate plus an amended report is two instruments. budget it, and do not
treat a total that exceeds the published schedule by a multiple of $40 as an
error on their side — under (c)(2) an under-tender is a non-filing, so this
matters to whether the paper lands at all.

🚩 **(c)(7) also names § 136** — a resignation of an agent without a successor,
one of the exemptions from this fee. § 136 is on the open-flag list at the foot
of this stone and remains unread.

### 🚨 HOW it may be signed — § 116(b) pulls SoS filings out of the e-signature harbor

before *who* signs, settle *how*. § 116 is the DGCL's electronic-signature
provision, and its subsection (a) is broad — an electronic transmission "shall be
deemed the equivalent of a written document," and a signature "may be a manual,
facsimile, conformed or **electronic** signature."

⛔ **then (b) takes two whole document classes back out, and they are the two
this route runs on:**

> "**(b) Subsection (a) of this section shall not apply to:**
>
> **(1) A document filed with or submitted to the Secretary of State**, the
> Register in Chancery, or a court or other judicial or governmental body of this
> State;
>
> **(2) A document comprising part of the stock ledger;** …
>
> (7) An act or transaction effected pursuant to § 280 of this title or
> subchapters III, XIII or XVI of this chapter."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 116(b), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc01/index.html

| the document | in the § 116(a) harbor? | why it matters here |
|---|---|---|
| the **Certificate of Renewal** | ⛔ **no** — (b)(1) | it is filed with the Secretary of State |
| a **notarized attestation emailed to the Division** | ⛔ **no** — (b)(1) reaches *"submitted to"*, not only *"filed with"* | this is the substantiation path |
| the **stock ledger** and its parts | ⛔ **no** — (b)(2) | it is your proof of issued shares |
| ✅ **the revival itself** | n/a — **(b)(7) does NOT reach it** | (b)(7) names subchapters III, XIII and **XVI**; XVI is **Foreign Corporations**. revival is § 312, subchapter XII |

⚠️ **(b)(7) was checked, not assumed.** a file on disk named `sc16.revival` in
fact held **Subchapter XVI — Foreign Corporations, §§ 371–385**. a filename is a
claim by whoever typed it; the subchapter title is the fact.

### ⛔ but (b) does NOT say "electronic is invalid" — read its final sentence

this is the whole discipline of the section, and it cuts against the dire read as
hard as it cuts against the casual one:

> "The foregoing shall **not create any presumption** about the lawful means to
> document a matter addressed by this subsection, or the lawful means to sign or
> deliver a document addressed by this subsection."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 116(b), final paragraph

| what (b) does | what (b) does NOT do |
|---|---|
| withdraws the § 116(a) **safe harbor** for these documents | ⛔ forbid electronic signature on them |
| leaves the question to other law | ⛔ create a presumption in **either** direction |

🎯 **so § 116 does not answer the question — it tells you § 116 is the wrong
place to look.** the answer for a Secretary of State filing comes from § 103's
execution and acknowledgment requirements and from the office's own filing
practice, and for the stock ledger from § 224.

⚠️ **that is exactly why this belongs on the call list rather than in a
conclusion here.** a cryptographic signature, a scanned wet-ink original, and a
typed-name-in-cursive are three different instruments, and § 116 declines to rank
them. ask which the office accepts for the certificate, and do not infer it from
the fact that the Division accepted a PDF by email — acceptance of a
**substantiation document** is not acceptance of a **filed certificate**.

### ⭐ who may sign — § 312(h) deems the board, and reaches § 502(a) too

a void corporation has an obvious problem: its directors' authority ran through
a charter that no longer stands. § 312(h) fixes it by deeming:

> "**For purposes of this section and § 502(a) of this title**, the board of
> directors ... **shall be comprised of the persons, who, but for the
> certificate of incorporation having become forfeited or void ..., would be the
> duly elected or appointed directors** ... The requirement for authorization by
> the board of directors under subsection (c) ... shall be satisfied if a
> majority of the directors ... then in office, even though less than a quorum,
> **or the sole director** ... then in office, authorizes the revival ..."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(h)

⭐ **read the opening words: "and § 502(a) of this title."** § 502(a) is the
**annual franchise tax report**. so the same deemed-board rule that lets a sole
director authorize the revival **also supplies the authority to sign the amended
reports** at steps 2 and 3 — while the charter is still void.

🎯 **that is the statutory footing under the Division's own instruction to file
amended reports before revival.** the agency's sequence and the statute's
sequence agree, and (h) is why the first half is lawful.

⚠️ **if there are NO directors**, (h) has a path and it is not a shortcut: the
**stockholders elect a full board**, by a special meeting called *"by any officer
or stockholder upon notice given in accordance with § 222."* the bylaws that
govern are *"the bylaws ... that, but for the ... void, would be the duly adopted
bylaws."*

### ⚠️ before you file — TWO checks § 312 imposes that no other step will

| check | § | what happens if you skip it |
|---|---|---|
| **is the name still available?** | **(f)** | if another Delaware corporation, or a § 371-qualified foreign corporation, took the name or one *"so nearly similar thereto as not to distinguish it"*, you **may not revive under the old name** — the certificate must carry a **new** one |
| **was the charter revoked under § 284?** | **(b)** | ⛔ § 312 *"shall not be applicable"*. a § 284 revocation is an Attorney General proceeding, not a tax void — a different status with no revival under this section |

✅ **and the good news in (b): there is no deadline.** *"may **at any time**
procure a revival."* the cost grows (§ 312(g) triples past 5 years), but the
right does not expire.

### ⭐ "at any time" is not a figure of speech — § 311 is the proof

a reader with an old void will not take *"at any time"* at face value, and they
are right to be skeptical: the section next door puts a **hard clock** on the
other route back into existence.

> "(a) **At any time prior to the expiration of 3 years following the
> dissolution** of a corporation pursuant to § 275 of this title ... **or at any
> time prior to the expiration of 3 years following the expiration of the time
> limited for the corporation's existence** as provided in its certificate of
> incorporation ..., a corporation may revoke the dissolution theretofore
> effected by it or **restore its certificate of incorporation** after it has
> expired by its own limitation ..."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 311(a), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

read the two sections side by side. they sit one apart in the same subchapter,
they both put a lapsed corporation back into existence, and the legislature gave
them **opposite** time rules:

| the route back | how the corporation left | the window |
|---|---|---|
| **§ 311** — revoke a dissolution, or restore an expired charter | a **voluntary** act, or a term that ran out | ⛔ **3 years**, hard |
| **§ 312** — revival of a forfeited or void charter | a **tax** default | ✅ **"at any time"** |

⭐ **that contrast is the strongest available proof that § 312's phrase means
what it says.** the drafter who wrote *"at any time"* in § 312(b) wrote *"prior
to the expiration of 3 years"* one section earlier — so the absence of a clock in
§ 312 is a **choice**, not an oversight.

⚠️ **and it tells you which section you are in.** if the corporation dissolved
voluntarily, or its charter carried a term that expired, you are on the § 311
route and the 3-year clock is real. § 312 reaches the **tax** void, and only it.

---

## fees

every figure below is the `Revival` row of Delaware's own fee schedule,
[revised 2026-08-01](https://corpfiles.delaware.gov/Fee_Schedule/AugustFee2026.pdf).

| item | cost | schedule row |
|------|------|--------------|
| Certificate of Revival | **$189.00** | `Revival (plus taxes to renew)`, column 1 |
| Certificate of Revival — **exempt corporation** | **$5.00** | `Revival (exempt)`, column 1 |
| certified copy (each) | +$50.00 | column 2 |
| same-day service | +**$200.00** | column 3 |
| 24-hour service | +$100.00 | column 4 |
| Priority 2 (2-hour) | +$500.00 | schedule footer |
| Priority 1 (1-hour) | +$1000.00 | schedule footer |
| each additional page | +$9.00 | the schedule's additional-page note |

> ```
> Revival (plus taxes to renew)      $189.00     $50.00     $200.00     $100.00
> Revival (exempt)                     $5.00     $50.00     $200.00     $100.00
> ```
> — [DE Division of Corporations, Fee Schedule, revised 2026-08-01](https://corpfiles.delaware.gov/Fee_Schedule/AugustFee2026.pdf)

> ✅ **the $189 is CORROBORATED by a second instrument already on disk**, and it
> is the better of the two — the cover letter Delaware ships **with the revival
> form itself**:
>
> > "The fee to file the Certificate is **$189.00**. If the document is more than
> > 1 page, please include **$9.00** for each additional page. You will receive a
> > stamped "Filed" copy of the submitted document. A certified copy may be
> > requested for an additional **$50**."
> >
> > — DE Division of Corporations, cover letter to the Certificate of Revival of
> > Charter (voided corporation), `.scratch/refs/pulls/form.revival.void.txt`,
> > 5,382 chars, pulled via bhrowser
>
> ⭐ that instrument is **narrower and therefore stronger**: a fee chart states
> the price of every certificate, and a reader must pick the right row; this
> letter states the price of **this one certificate** and cannot be misread onto
> a neighbor. the primary fee, the additional-page fee, and the certified-copy
> fee all agree with the table above.
>
> ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-14 — the schedule is embedded.** the PDF was fetched as
> bytes through the browser's request context, its `%PDF` magic confirmed, and
> `pdftotext -layout` run over it. both artifacts sit beside this brief at
> `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/effectiveAt=2026-08-01.de-doc-fee-schedule.{pdf,txt}`
> — 254,794 bytes, extract 34,625 chars, `application/pdf`.
>
> the playbooks that earned the url and fetched it are kept beside them, so
> every figure here is re-derivable rather than merely asserted:
> `registrator/briefs/refs/delaware-fee-schedule.{nav,pull}.play.ts`.
>
> ⭐ **the revision was read off the DOCUMENT, never off the slug.** the pull
> reports every date-like token in the body, and the body returned exactly one:
>
> > `DELAWARE DEPARTMENT OF STATE   Revised August 1, 2026`
>
> the slug `AugustFee2026.pdf` agrees with it — but the agreement is the *result*
> of the check, not a substitute for it. **a stale fee schedule has bitten this
> matter before**, and in the direction that punishes the careful reader: a pull
> that succeeded against an old revision retracted a correct $189 and shipped
> that retraction as verified. a slug that looks current is what made that
> possible.
>
> ⛔ **so the $189 is now settled three ways** — the schedule row, the cover
> letter Delaware ships with the form, and the retraction above, which this pull
> refutes. **$189.00 is correct and the retraction was the defect.**

#### ⛔ two traps in this schedule that a read of the table alone will not catch

**1. the fastest service is NOT in a column.** the table carries two expedite
columns, `Same Day Fee` and `24-Hour Fee`. two faster tiers exist and live in a
footnote *below* the table, in a **different unit**:

> "Priority 1 (1-Hour) Service is available at a cost of **$1000 per
> document/request** and Priority 2 (2-Hour) Service is available at a cost of
> **$500 per document/request**."
>
> — DE Division of Corporations, Fee Schedule, revised 2026-08-01

a reader who consults only the columns concludes that same-day at $200 is the
fastest option Delaware offers. it is not. and the unit differs: the column fees
attach to the certificate, the Priority fees are **per document/request**.

**2. the two tables do not share an axis.** the corporate table runs four
columns; the certificates table on the later page runs **three** — it has no
certified-copy column at all:

```
corporate     Certificate Type | State Filing Fee | Certified Copy | Same Day | 24-Hour
certificates  Certificate Type | Fee              | Same Day       | 24-Hour
```

⚠️ carry the first header across to the second and every figure lands one column
to the left — a good-standing same-day fee reads as a certified-copy fee. every
cell stays true and the row becomes false. **read the header that sits above the
row you are in, not the one you last looked at.**

⚠️ note also that the expedite columns run **fastest first**, so the left of the
two is the *more* expensive one. a reader who assumes the cheaper comes first
swaps $200 and $100, and the swap is invisible because both numbers are real and
both sit on the row.

### ⚠️ read the row's own parenthetical: "plus taxes to renew"

the schedule says the fee is **not** the bill, and § 312(g)
says which taxes it means — including one the § 312(g) cap does **not** cover:

> "No payment made pursuant to this subsection **shall reduce the amount of
> franchise tax due under Chapter 5 of this title for the year in which the
> revival is effected**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(g), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

⭐ **so the CURRENT year is a separate bill.** § 312(g)'s cap — *"all franchise
taxes, penalties and interest thereon due at the time its certificate of
incorporation became forfeited or void"* — looks backward to the void date. this
sentence says the year you revive in stands on its own and is **not** reduced by
a dollar of that payment.

| what | covered by the § 312(g) payment? |
|---|---|
| delinquent years, up to the **void date** | ✅ yes — that is the cap |
| ⛔ **the year the revival is effected** | **no.** owed separately, in full, under chapter 5 |
| the $189 filing fee | no — a fee, not a tax |

🎯 **budget three lines, not one:** the back taxes, the current year's franchise
tax, and the filing fee. a plan that budgets only the arrears will be short by a
whole year's tax at the worst moment.

⚠️ and the current year's tax has its **own** § 503(a)(1)/(a)(2) election — if
the report for it is absent, it too defaults to Authorized Shares.

> 🚨 **this table read `+$100` for same-day and had no exempt row.**
>
> the `$189` was right. the **expedite figures were not**: `+$100` is the
> **24-hour** fee, and same-day is **$200.00**. so a filer who paid for
> same-day would have been short by half.
>
> ⚠️ **and the `$5.00` exempt rate was absent entirely** — a 38× cheaper path
> for a corporation that qualifies. a stone that omits an option does not read
> as incomplete; it reads as though the option does not exist.
>
> ⭐ **the same defect, with the same shape, was live in the neighbor stone**
> `corp.shares.reduce/templates/3.file.amendment.stone`: right primary fee,
> wrong expedite column. the expedite columns are the part nobody re-checks,
> because the headline number is correct.
>
> ⛔ **do not read one row's expedite fees onto another.** they genuinely vary
> by certificate type — `Incorporation` is `$100.00` same-day / `$50.00`
> 24-hour, while `Revival` and `Amendment` are `$200.00` / `$100.00`. read the
> row for the certificate you actually file.

