## timeline

| option | duration | added fee |
|--------|----------|-----------|
| standard (Priority 7 — Reg. Work) | ⛔ **none is published** — call for the current processed date | — |
| 24-hour | 1 business day | +$100.00 |
| same day | same business day | +**$200.00** |
| Priority 2 (2-hour) | 2 hours | +$500.00 |
| Priority 1 (1-hour) | 1 hour | +$1000.00 |

fees per the `## fees` table above.

⭐ **the Division's substitute for a duration is a phone call**, and the question
has a specific shape:

> "you may contact our office to find out **what date is currently being
> processed for regular work**"
>
> — the FAQ, `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/pulledAt=2026-09-25.de-faq-regular-work-turnaround.txt`

⇒ ask **"what date are you at?"**, never **"how long will mine take?"** the first
is a fact already on a clerk's screen; the second asks them to forecast their own
queue, which is the forecast the FAQ declines to put in print.

⭐ and the expedite choice is **not locked at submit time** — an item already in
the regular queue can be bought forward by a call. so Priority 7 plus a call is
the correct first move on a filing with no hard deadline.

> 🚨 **this row said `2-3 weeks` until 2026-09-25, and its hedge had gone stale in
> the FRIENDLY direction — which is why it survived so long.** the hedge read
> *"not from the fee schedule and remains unsourced — treat it as an estimate"*:
> an honest caveat about a number, that still hands the reader a number to plan
> on.
>
> the source now exists, and it supplies no interval. it declines to:
>
> > "Processing time for **regular (non-expedited) work varies based upon the
> > volume of items received** by our office."
> >
> > — the FAQ, `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/pulledAt=2026-09-25.de-faq-regular-work-turnaround.txt`
>
> ⇒ so the defect was never an absent citation. **there is no interval to cite**,
> and a reader who trusted the estimate would plan against a date the Division
> never offered.
>
> ⇒ the walk of three documents, the embedded bytes, and the verifier that
> clamps every span:
> `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/howto.submit-a-certificate-of-revival.md`

> 🚨 **this table listed COSTS in a column headed `duration`, and had no
> durations in it at all.**
>
> three of its four rows were dollar figures, one of them wrong: same-day read
> `+$100` where the schedule says **$200.00**. so the table answered neither
> question it appeared to answer — a reader who wanted a turnaround time found
> a price, and a reader who wanted a price found a wrong one.
>
> ⭐ **the identical defect was live in `corp.shares.reduce/templates/3.file.amendment.stone`**
> — a `timeline` table whose duration column held costs. two stones, two
> routes, one shape.
>
> ⛔ **and this is the third time in one file-fix pass that a correction landed
> on the first table and missed a second one further down.** the `## fees`
> section was rewritten from the schedule; this table, under `## timeline`,
> said `+$100` for the same service and was caught only by a re-grep after the
